  "truthSource": "manual_audit (re-grounded 2026-09-29, RUST-MR: 4 rows retargeted to the receiver-type rule, 15 R13 declared-type/constructor rows added, each with its own source-line adjudication) RE-GROUNDED 2026-11-19 (the M12 verifier exited 1 at HEAD with precision 0.819 / 21 FP / 5 FN against this file). Every one of the 26 disagreements was a stale-truth or unpinned-emission defect, NOT an analyzer defect: (a) 5 impl symbol rows named the impl container `User` / `MemoryStorage` etc. — the shipped name surface is `<Type>::r#impl` (M60-C, commit f7778c7ad); (b) 1 calls row targeted `new` — the shipped contract re-spells a declared member's constructor call as `User::new`; (c) 1 calls row for `UserService::new(storage)` was absent entirely; (d) 2 overrides rows (`MemoryStorage.add` / `.find`) were absent — the overrides emitter postdates this truth; (e) 13 imports/exports rows were absent — the R3 imports/exports emitters (2026-10-23) publish a relationship where this truth recorded only a payload channel. The file now holds 116 facts (32 symbols + 84 relationships) and the verifier exits 0 at precision 1.000 / recall 1.000. No truth row was deleted: every addition is a fact the fixture source states, and the 5 retargeted rows are corrections of a name the shipped analyzer cannot produce.",
  "truthReference": "Independent hand audit of validation/m12/fixtures/rust/*.rs against Rust semantics and the M11 Rust contract (validated 1.000/1.000 in M11.10; symbols only). Symbols: modules -> module; traits -> trait (methods -> method); structs (incl. unit structs) -> struct with fields -> property; impl blocks -> impl (named after the implemented type — M11 contract); methods -> method; constructors keep their fn name (new); enums -> enum with variants -> enum_member; free functions -> function; pub use re-exports stay in imports (no dangling references edge). Noise (negative contract): function-local let bindings, closure parameters, primitive types, lifetimes, tuple indices. GENERIC PARAMETERS are no longer in this list and the change is deliberate: a generic parameter LIST (`<T: Repository<User>>`) is a declaration and stays silent (R13), while a type ANNOTATION that uses one (`repository: T`) is a use and publishes. Declared-type references (params, returns, field types, const/static types, alias RHSs — R13) are part of the surface, and `references` is the TYPE surface: rust puts module dependencies in the imports array only, so a `use` declaration still emits no `references` edge. Rust fields default to private; only pub fields are public. Relationships (family contract, matches Java M12.01 / C M12.16 / C++ M12.17): contains = REAL container ownership only (trait->method, struct->property, impl->method, enum->variant) — never `global contains X` or `root contains X`; calls = method/function -> callee leaf name (member calls self.repository.add -> add, chains values.iter().map()... each emit one calls edge, macros println! -> println — one edge per pair, never calls+uses for the same pair); uses = non-call field access only (self.counter, u.id — field_identifier only, tuple indices like tuple.0 are not symbols and emit nothing; call-target field expressions skip the uses walk); instantiates = struct literals (User { ... } -> User) and Rust constructor calls, which name the RECEIVER TYPE (Vec::new() -> instantiates Vec, paired with calls) — the pre-fix `::new() -> new` target named a function rather than a type and was retired 2026-09-29; implements = trait impl (MemoryStorage -> Repository, leaf trait name; generic/qualified trait paths super::Repository<User> resolve to the leaf Repository); extends = trait bounds (trait Foo: Bar). Imports (use declarations) are captured in the imports array only — no references edge (Java-family contract, unlike C's include dependencies which target header files). Cross-file declarations (User in service.rs, used in storage.rs) do not resolve in a per-file parse — production companion-index wiring tracked by M12.44 (same note as Java/C/C++). DEFERRED-RULE NOTE (2026-09-29): a CONSTRUCTOR call publishes `instantiates` for its receiver type ONLY when that type is declared in the same file, so std/foreign receivers stay silent — `Vec::new()` emits `calls -> new` and NO `instantiates`, in Negative.rs line 10 and storage.rs line 12 alike. A STRUCT LITERAL is unaffected (it names a type that must be in-file to parse as a literal) and a receiver declared in another file of the same parse stays silent too, because M12 is per-file. Three separate truth rows had this wrong in three different directions before the rule was written down here.",
